    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. I've been seeing some significant variations in the distribution of the temper, too. In some samples, the inclusions seem almost aggressively scattered or unevenly clumped within the matrix, rather than being a controlled part of the paste. Does that kind of irregular distribution usually point to a specific type of primitive kiln, or could it just be an issue with how the vessels were handled or broken up after firing?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Irregularly clumped temper often suggests inconsistent mixing of the clay body before forming, rather than being solely a kiln artifact. Primitive kilns can exacerbate this by creating uneven temperature gradients, causing parts of the vessel to mature differently. However, if the clumping is severe and localized across multiple pieces, it might suggest poor preparation of the raw materials or inadequate thorough mixing in the production phase.",
      "source": "target",
